==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{proto|Germanic|skrīþan|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|(s)kreit-||(s)ker-|twist, turn, bend|lang=ang}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Saxon ''scrîðan'' (Dutch ''[[schrijden]]''), Old High German ''scrîtan'' (German ''[[schreiten]]''), Old Norse ''skríða'' ‘creep, glide, [[scree]]’ (Swedish ''[[skrida]]'').  The Indo-European root also gave Latin ''[[crisare]]'' and Middle Irish {{tø|mga|crith|xs=Middle Irish}} (Breton ''[[skrija]]'' ‘tremble with fear’).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃri:ðɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{Latinx|'''scrīþan'''}}

# to [[move]], [[glide]], [[wander]]

